@@ -0,0 +1,156 @@
+# Correlic benchmarks
+
+Reproducible measurements of what the Linux agent costs on a host and what the
+telemetry plane can ingest. The committed numbers live in `results/` and are
+interpreted in [`backend/docs/BENCHMARKS.md`](../../backend/docs/BENCHMARKS.md).
+
+Two scripts, one Go helper:
+
+| Script | Measures |
+|---|---|
+| `agent-overhead.sh` | Agent CPU % and RSS, eBPF kernel time per program, dispatcher drops, events that reached the backend, and the whole-machine delta (workload completion, total CPU) with vs. without the agent, under idle/light/moderate/heavy synthetic workloads. |
+| `backend-ingest.sh` | Telemetry plane `/ingest/events` throughput (accepted events/s), HTTP p50/p95/p99, plane CPU/RSS, PostgreSQL CPU and growth, detection findings, with the sampler on and off. |
+| `loadgen/` | Self-contained Go module (`loadgen` binary): workload generator, local TCP/UDP sinks, `/proc` + eBPF sampler, ingest load generator, agent-log parser, report aggregator. Not part of the agent or backend modules. |
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+- Linux 5.8+ with BTF (`/sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux`), x86_64 or arm64, run as **root**
+  (the agent needs CAP_BPF/CAP_PERFMON; the script toggles
+  `/proc/sys/kernel/bpf_stats_enabled` and restores it).
+- Go 1.26+, clang + libbpf headers if the agent's eBPF objects are not generated
+  yet (`BENCH_LIBBPF_INCLUDE=<dir with bpf/bpf_helpers.h>`), `psql`,
+  `pg_isready`, `curl`, `setsid`.
+- PostgreSQL 14+ reachable with a role that may `CREATE DATABASE`
+  (`BENCH_PG_HOST`, `BENCH_PG_PORT`, `BENCH_PG_USER`, `BENCH_PG_PASSWORD`;
+  default `127.0.0.1:5432`, user `correlic`). The scripts create and drop
+  `bench_agent` / `bench_ingest`.
+- Free ports: `BENCH_API_PORT` (default 38080), `BENCH_TELEMETRY_PORT` (38081),
+  and UDP `127.0.0.1:53` for the DNS sink of the agent benchmark (stop a local
+  resolver such as systemd-resolved on that address first, or the script stops
+  with a clear message).
+- A quiet machine: nothing else CPU-heavy, no other Correlic agent.
+
+Everything is built from the checkout into `BENCH_WORK/bin`
+(`BENCH_WORK` defaults to `/var/tmp/correlic-bench`; it must not be under
+`/tmp`, which the agent's path filter ignores, and must not contain a path
+component that matches an AI pattern such as `claude-...`, or the agent would
+attribute every benchmark process to that tool — the scripts check both); the
+planes run with a
+throwaway org, keys and mTLS certificates generated by `correlic-admin
+bootstrap` under `BENCH_WORK/*-certs`, which are never committed.
+
+## Running
+
+```bash
+# agent overhead: 4 scenarios x {agent, no agent} x 3 repetitions x 60 s  (~35 min)
+sudo scripts/bench/agent-overhead.sh
+# telemetry ingest: 5 configurations x 3 repetitions x 30 s  (~10 min)
+sudo scripts/bench/backend-ingest.sh
+```
+
+Options: `--duration`, `--reps`, `--warmup`, `--scenarios idle,light,...`,
+`--ai-share` (agent), `--concurrency 1,8,32`, `--batch 100` (ingest), `--out DIR`
+for the summary files. A quick smoke test:
+`agent-overhead.sh --duration 5 --reps 1 --scenarios light`.
+
+`agent-overhead.sh` refuses to start from a shell that descends from a process
+named like an AI coding tool (`claude`, `cursor`, `aider`, ...), because the
+agent would attribute the whole benchmark, planes included, to that AI session
+(the same happens when the launching shell's command line contains such a
+name, e.g. a path under `~/.claude`). Use `--detach`: the script re-launches
+itself with `setsid -f`, is reparented to pid 1, and logs to
+`$BENCH_WORK/agent-overhead.log`:
+
+```bash
+sudo scripts/bench/agent-overhead.sh --detach; tail -f /var/tmp/correlic-bench/agent-overhead.log
+```
+
+The file-open generator writes its 64 files under `/home/correlic-bench`
+(`BENCH_FILES_DIR`), because the agent's file-open BPF program only emits opens
+of paths under `/home/` or of credential-like paths; the directory is removed
+at exit.
+
+Both scripts stop every process they started (agent, planes, sinks) on exit,
+also on Ctrl-C, and remove their temp files.
+
+## What the agent benchmark does
+
+For each scenario, repetition and mode (`agent` = agent running, `noagent` =
+not running), in interleaved order:
+
+1. With `agent`: start `correlic-agent` against the local planes, wait
+   `--warmup` seconds (startup `/proc` scan, pattern fetch).
+2. Start `loadgen monitor` for `--duration` seconds: every second it reads
+   `utime+stime`, `VmRSS`, `VmHWM` of the agent and both planes, sums all
+   `postgres` processes, reads `/proc/stat`, and at start/end snapshots
+   `run_cnt`/`run_time_ns` of every loaded eBPF program (`bpf_stats_enabled=1`).
+3. Start two `loadgen workload` generators for the same duration: one inside a
+   fake AI tree (`exec -a claude bash -c ...`, so its argv[0] matches the seeded
+   AI pattern the way the real CLI does), one plain "noise" tree. Each gets
+   `--ai-share` (default 50 %) of the scenario's rates:
+
+   | Scenario | exec /s | file open /s | TCP connect /s | DNS send /s |
+   |---|---|---|---|---|
+   | idle | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
+   | light | 10 | 10 | 5 | 5 |
+   | moderate | 100 | 100 | 50 | 20 |
+   | heavy | 1000 | 500 | 200 | 50 |
+
+   exec = `/bin/true`; file = open+close of 64 files in a temp dir; connect =
+   TCP connect+close to a local listener; dns = one DNS A-query datagram to
+   `127.0.0.1:53`. Each generator is paced; ticks that find every worker busy
+   are counted as *missed*, so `completion %` is honest.
+4. With `agent`: wait for the dispatcher's next 30 s drop-stats log line, stop
+   the agent, parse its log (`rate_limited`, `buffer_full`, `deduplicated`,
+   `exec_cleanup`, WARN/ERROR).
+5. Count the events stored in the `events` table before/after.
+
+Raw per-run files go to `BENCH_WORK/agent-overhead/runs/<scenario>-<mode>-r<rep>/`
+(`monitor.json`, `workload-ai.json`, `workload-noise.json`, `agentlog.json`,
+`db.json`, `meta.json`, `agent.log`). `loadgen report -kind agent` reduces them
+to the median/min/max tables in `results/agent-overhead.{json,md}`.
+
+## What the ingest benchmark does
+
+`loadgen ingest` posts batches of 100 synthetic canonical events (30 %
+`process_exec`, 50 % `file_open`, 20 % `net_connect`, same field layout as the
+agent's runners, 1 % of them credential-path / external-C2-shaped so detection
+rules fire) with the agent key over mTLS from N concurrent senders for 30 s
+after a 2 s warm-up. Configurations: AI-tagged events (what the Linux agent
+actually sends) at concurrency 1, 8 and 32 with the default sampler, then
+non-AI events at the middle concurrency with the sampler on
+(`noise-c8-sampled`) and off (`noise-c8-unsampled`, planes restarted with
+`SAMPLING_ENABLED=false`). The planes run with `CORRELIC_DISABLE_RATE_LIMIT=1`,
+otherwise one key is capped at 300 requests/min. The monitor samples the
+telemetry plane, the API plane, the load generator and all PostgreSQL
+processes; table sizes and row counts of `telemetry_events`, `events`,
+`findings`, `incidents` are read before and after each run.
+
+Raw files: `BENCH_WORK/backend-ingest/runs/<label>-r<rep>/`; summary:
+`results/backend-ingest.{json,md}`.
+
+## Results layout
+
+- `results/agent-overhead.json`, `results/backend-ingest.json`: environment
+  (`kernel`, `cpu_model`, `ncpu`, `mem_total_kb`, `go_version`, `commit`,
+  `container`), the run configuration, and per scenario/configuration every
+  metric as `{median, min, max, n, values}` across repetitions.
+- `results/*.md`: the same as tables (what `BENCHMARKS.md` quotes).
+- Raw logs and per-run JSON are not committed; they stay under `BENCH_WORK`.
+
+## Reproducing on bare metal
+
+The committed numbers come from a cloud container (see `environment` in the
+JSON). On your own hardware:
+
+1. Use a dedicated machine or VM with nothing else running; disable CPU
+   frequency scaling / turbo if you want run-to-run spread below a few percent
+   (`cpupower frequency-set -g performance`).
+2. Install Go 1.26+, clang, libbpf headers, PostgreSQL 14+; create a
+   `correlic` role with `CREATEDB`.
+3. `export BENCH_PG_USER=... BENCH_PG_PASSWORD=...`; run both scripts as root
+   (detached, see above); copy the two files from `results/` into your report.
+4. For a laptop-like picture, also run the agent next to your real editor and
+   compare `agent-overhead.json` → `scenarios[].with_agent.agent_cpu_pct`
+   against `top`/`ps` of the agent over a working session: the synthetic
+   workload is deliberately busier than an interactive session.
